      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bow Mar’s name honors pioneering farmers Joseph Bowles and John Marston, whose early homesteads shaped the area’s rural roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_Mar%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially incorporated in 1958, straddling both Arapahoe and Jefferson counties as a Colorado statutory town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_Mar%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early homes in Bow Mar reflected Frank Lloyd Wright’s prairie-style architecture, with wide streets and single‑story lots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_Mar%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lloyd and Eleanor King built the first house in Bow Mar in 1947, marking the start of residential development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_Mar%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Despite its growth, Bow Mar never had its own post office, relying on nearby towns for mail services.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_Mar%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Bow Mar is part of the Denver‑Aurora‑Centennial Metropolitan Statistical Area, linking it to the larger Front Range urban corridor.",
